\title[Adaptive Smoothness Bounds Are Oracle-Only]{Adaptive Smoothness Bounds in the Quadratic Sieve:\\ A Real Per-Instance Optimum That Only an Oracle Can Reach}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{October 21, 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We study whether the smoothness bound $B$ of the single-polynomial quadratic sieve should be chosen per instance. In an experiment comprising $1{,}800$ complete sieve runs---$120$ validated $41$-bit balanced semiprimes, each run at all $15$ values of a ladder $B\in[150,19000]$ with fixed sieve length $M=150{,}000$---we price each run by its \emph{paid touch}, the number of elementary sieve operations actually executed. We prove that paid touch equals $2M\sum_{p\in S,\,p\text{ odd}}1/p+M/2$ up to an additive error of at most $2|S|-1$, where $S$ is the factor base, and observe a maximum relative deviation of $2.01\times10^{-4}$. The per-instance optimum $B^{*}$ spans the whole ladder (median $4800$). An oracle selecting $B^{*}$ per instance reduces work to $0.9533\times$ that of the best fixed bound ($B=9600$), improving $102/120$ instances and worsening none ($t=-11.6$). Yet no rule based on features computable before sieving recovers this gain on held-out data: a stratified rule achieves $1.0047\times$ and a regression rule $1.0290\times$, against an oracle of $0.9590\times$ on the same split; the prize ($0.0478$ log units) is dwarfed by the held-out residual standard deviation ($0.536$), a signal-to-noise ratio of $0.089$. We explain these findings by an exact theory of oracle, fixed and feature-based selection: the ordering oracle $\le$ best feature rule $\le$ best fixed value, attainment by per-fibre minimisation, an exact zero-gain criterion, in-sample refinement monotonicity, and an explicit train/test reversal. Finally we show that the observed elasticity $d\log Q/d\log m=0.99718\pm0.03722$ is, up to $O(c/Q)$, a bookkeeping identity: if $m=Q+c$ then every secant elasticity lies in $[1,1+c/Q_1]$.
\end{abstract}

